---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const withBase = (p = '') => (base.endsWith('/') ? base : base + '/') + p;
---

<Guide
  title="What the Bhagavad Gita says about overthinking and the restless mind"
  description="The Bhagavad Gita calls the mind restless and hard to hold — and gives a precise method for a mind that won't stop. Verses 6.26, 6.35, 2.67 and 2.70, explained."
  slug="bhagavad-gita-on-overthinking"
  datePublished="2026-10-17"
  faq={[
    {
      q: 'What does the Bhagavad Gita say about overthinking?',
      a: 'The Gita agrees the mind is restless and hard to control (6.35), but says it can be trained. The method in 6.26 is simple and repeatable: each time the mind wanders off, gently bring it back — again and again — rather than fighting it or giving up.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How does the Bhagavad Gita say to stop a restless mind?',
      a: 'Through two things named in 6.35 — abhyasa (steady practice) and vairagya (non-attachment) — and the technique of 6.26: notice the wandering and lead the mind back to steadiness, patiently, however many times it strays. The Gita treats calm as trained, not forced.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Which Gita verse is about controlling the mind?',
      a: 'Verse 6.35 calls the mind restless but masterable by practice and non-attachment. Verse 6.26 gives the method of gently returning it whenever it wanders. Verse 2.67 warns how a single pursued distraction can carry judgment away like wind carries a boat.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does the Gita say the mind can really be controlled?',
      a: 'It is honest that the mind is "as hard to hold as the wind" (6.34), but Krishna does not concede defeat: it is hard, he says, yet mastered by practice and non-attachment (6.35). Difficulty is acknowledged; impossibility is denied.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    When Krishna tells Arjuna to steady his mind in meditation, Arjuna pushes back with a line anyone
    who has lain awake at 3am will recognise: the mind, he says, is restless, turbulent, strong and
    stubborn — trying to hold it is like trying to hold the wind. The remarkable thing is that Krishna
    does not argue. He agrees. And then he says it can be done anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    That exchange, in <a href={withBase('gita/6')}>Chapter 6</a>, is the Gita's direct address to
    overthinking — the looping, the rehearsing, the 2am courtroom where you argue a case nobody is
    hearing. Its answer is less a single insight than a practice.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest diagnosis</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"The mind is restless, turbulent, strong and unyielding; I think it as hard to master as the wind."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/6/34')}>Bhagavad Gita 6.34</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    It matters that this is Arjuna's honest complaint and that the Gita lets it stand. You are not
    failing at something that is supposed to be easy. The mind is genuinely hard to hold — the text
    says so plainly before it offers any remedy.
  </p>

  <h2>Hard, but not hopeless</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Without doubt the mind is restless and hard to hold; but by practice and by non-attachment it can be mastered."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/6/35')}>Bhagavad Gita 6.35</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>Practice</strong> (<em>abhyasa</em>) is the patient, repeated return to steadiness.
    <strong>Non-attachment</strong> (<em>vairagya</em>) is loosening the grip on the outcomes the mind
    keeps circling. Overthinking almost never yields to a single clever thought — if it did, you'd have
    thought your way out already. It yields to this: quiet, unglamorous, repeated practice.
  </p>

  <h2>The actual technique</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Whenever the restless, unsteady mind wanders away, let one draw it back and bring it under the control of the self."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/6/26')}>Bhagavad Gita 6.26</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    This is the whole method, and it is strikingly close to what modern mindfulness teaches. You do not
    stop the mind from wandering — that is not on offer. You notice that it has wandered, and you lead
    it back, <em>gently</em>, without the second spiral of scolding yourself for wandering. The returning
    <em>is</em> the practice. Each time counts, even the hundredth time in a minute.
  </p>

  <h2>Why one distraction becomes a flood</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"As a boat on the water is carried away by a gale, so even one of the wandering senses, if the mind follows it, carries away one's judgment."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/67')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.67</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The Gita's warning is precise: it is <em>following</em> the stray thought that does the damage. The
    thought arrives on its own; the overthinking begins when the mind climbs aboard and lets itself be
    carried. Catching that first boarding — before the gale picks up — is where the work is easiest.
  </p>

  <h2>The image to aim for</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"As the waters flow into the ocean, which is ever being filled yet remains unmoved, so the one whom all desires enter attains peace — not the one who chases after desires."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/70')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.70</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Notice the ocean does not refuse the rivers. Thoughts and desires still arrive; peace is not an
    empty mind but a steady one — large enough to receive the flow without being thrown by it. That,
    not a blank, is the stillness the Gita points you toward.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to read next</h2>
  <p>
    Sit with <a href={withBase('gita/6')}>Chapter 6</a> on meditation and the mind. If the overthinking
    is really anxious looping, see <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-anxiety')}>the Gita on
    anxiety</a>; if it keeps you flat and stuck, see
    <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-depression')}>the Gita on despair and feeling low</a>.
    Or open Gita Daily and let a verse meet your mind where it is right now.
  </p>
</Guide>
